[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The suspected physical failure on the router0 <-> router1 transit link did not occur. The affected component is not a cable or interface, but likely routing policy/config on router1. Direct evidence on the implicated interfaces shows the link is healthy:
- router0 eth2 (wan_link_r0_r1) is UP/LOWER_UP with IP 10.16.1.1; ethtool reports Link detected: yes, 10G, full-duplex; it pings router1 (10.16.1.2) successfully with 0% loss.
- router1 eth2 (wan_link_r0_r1) is UP/LOWER_UP with IP 10.16.1.2; ethtool reports Link detected: yes, 10G, full-duplex; it pings router0 (10.16.1.1) successfully with 0% loss.

Localization: the router0<->router1 link uses router0.eth2 and router1.eth2; both are operational. Several other interfaces on router1 (eth0, eth1, eth4, eth5) are DOWN, but these are not part of the r0<->r1 link. Mechanism for the reported “partial reachability” appears control-plane related: router0’s routing table shows multiple RIP-learned routes via 10.16.1.2 and 172.20.1.2, while router1’s table lacks comparable learned routes from router0 (only connected 10.16.1.0/24 and 10.20.1.0/24 and two next-hops via 10.20.1.2), indicating asymmetry after the change.

Root cause: No physical link failure on r0<->r1; the issue is likely a routing change on router1 preventing proper route propagation.

Corrective fix: Do not dispatch cabling work. Instead, review/rollback the recent router1 change and restore dynamic routing or static routes between zones (e.g., re-enable route advertisement toward 10.16.1.0/24), then verify end-to-end reachability.
=== END ===